// Title: Terminate Linux Process Via Kill
// Sigma ID: 64c41342-6b27-523b-5d3f-c265f3efcdb3
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Tuan Le (NCSGroup)
// Date: 2023-03-16 | Modified: 2024-12-12
// ATT&CK: defense-impairment, T1685
// Tags: detection.threat-hunting
// Description: Detects usage of command line tools such as "kill", "pkill" or "killall" to terminate or signal
//   a running process.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://www.trendmicro.com/en_us/research/23/c/iron-tiger-sysupdate-adds-linux-targeting.html
//   - https://www.cyberciti.biz/faq/how-force-kill-process-linux/
//   - https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/how-to-kill-processes-on-the-linux-desktop-with-xkill/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ccbb0369507f796db0b0ae6854c8a2be471b9d84/rules-threat-hunting/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_susp_process_termination_via_kill.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/kill" or FolderPath endswith "/killall" or FolderPath endswith "/pkill" or FolderPath endswith "/xkill"
